wishnode.net — verification receipts phext (phexts/verification.phext). Plain summary first (chapter 1); the full technical record of docs/VERIFICATION.md follows section by section from chapter 2. Source: Exocortexia repo docs/VERIFICATION.md at exo 9660d3b. Task 34.6.6/7.7.10/7.6.10.Read the full test results — Exocortexia | Phext, Inc.SUMMARY (plain English — rendered first)This page holds the proof for each claim on this site. When the suite was built, it was checked by 69 automated tests. All 69 passed. None failed. A security audit later the same day raised the count to 84 tests, and all 84 pass. The last two notes in this summary tell that part of the story.The tests cover each of the five programs and the shared core. They check that hiding text never changes the original record. They check that a changed entry in the record log is caught. They check that a form can never skip a step.The store was tested with 100,000 documents. It built the full set in about 70 milliseconds. Two hundred full searches took about 2.4 seconds in all. In office terms: a search of the whole set takes about a hundredth of a second.We ran a cheat test. We changed one past entry in the record log on purpose. The check caught the change. It caught it in use, and it caught it after the file was saved and read back. If a cheat like that is ever missed, the test run fails.All five programs were run and used by hand. Each home page loaded. Search worked. A bad hide request was refused. A good one worked, and the original stayed the same.Sign-in was tested with a test sign-in tool that acts like the real one. A true sign-in worked. A used sign-in code, used twice, was refused. A faked sign-in, with one letter changed, was refused.Speed was tested with 50 people at once, 1,000 requests each. There were no errors. The slowest groups of answers came back in 1 to 15 milliseconds — far inside the 500 millisecond limit we set.Then we pushed harder. When 500 people used one program at the same moment, answers slowed to about a second. We found the cause. The door that takes in new users had room for just 128 people in line. We made room for 1,024. The slowest answers then took about 26 milliseconds, with no errors. We kept the failed run in this record. The proof is the point.The sample files are made the same way each time. Make them again, and you get files that match, byte for byte. The match can be checked with a standard hash check (SHA-256).Honest limits: these speed tests ran on one computer on our own network, not on the public internet. The records demo holds 400 documents. The 100,000-document tests ran on the store itself, not in the demo program. This record is from a real run on 2026-11-07.One more run belongs in this story. After this record was made, a security audit checked the suite hard and found real holes. We fixed each hole, and we added a test that checks each fix: 15 new tests in all. Three guard the record log’s chain of proof. Four guard the way the programs take in requests and check where they came from. Four guard sign-in. One guards record search. Two guard forms. One guards the links the catalog checks.Our full check script ran again after the fixes, and it passed. It built the programs, ran all 84 tests, and made the sample files again — the new files matched the ones in this record, byte for byte. It ran the store tests with 100,000 documents again, too. The speed tests with hundreds of people at once were not run again; the ones in this record are the ones that stand. The audit’s full record is on this site, on the page called “Security.”Ask about a pilotAsk about a pilot for your office. Use the form on this site — the link says “Ask about a pilot.” Tell us which program you are interested in and what your office needs. When you send the form, you get a tracking code. Keep it. You can use it on this site to check the state of your request at any time. We read every request, and a person from Phext, Inc. replies. We will talk through what a small pilot would look like for your office. There is no cost to ask, and asking does not sign you up for anything.FULL TECHNICAL RECORD — Environment## Environment- Host: ranch node **aletheia-core** (x86_64, 16 cores, 29 GB RAM).
- Toolchain: cargo 1.94.0 / rustc 1.94.0.
- Phext library: **libphext 0.4.0 from crates.io** (the in-tree 0.4.2 on
  libphext-rs `exo` branches is not published to crates.io; 0.4.0 is what
  compiles cleanly and is what this suite uses — recorded per instructions).
- Thermal: node idled at Tctl ≈ 50 °C; peak observed 71.6 °C during the
  first full compile; the ≤ 75 °C sustained target held throughout.
- Other deps: sha2 0.10, hmac 0.12, base64 0.22, socket2 0.5 (see the
  backlog fix under Known failures — it was found and fixed by testing).FULL TECHNICAL RECORD — Build## Build`cargo build --workspace` (dev) and `cargo build --release --workspace`
both clean. Two warnings, both benign and left visible on purpose:- `exo-sso`: `value assigned to pos is never read` (flat-JSON parser).
- `minutes-publisher`: field `audit_store` never read after seeding (the
  `AuditLog` handle is what the routes use; the backing store struct
  field is retained for symmetry with records-desk).FULL TECHNICAL RECORD — Unit tests## Unit tests`cargo test --workspace`: **69 passed, 0 failed.**| Target | Passed |
